Two weeks ago, I had absolutely no idea how to build this automation.

And today, I have a fully functioning end-to-end solution running across Outlook, SharePoint, Power Automate, and Excel.

Not because I'm an expert.

Not because I was formally trained.

But because I've believed one thing: "Give me enough time, and I'll figure it out."



In the beginning it was simple:

Automate the collection of email data, store it centrally, process it automatically, calculate daily results, and send validated summaries to users.

Simple on paper.

A completely different story in reality.

What I didn't realize was how many moving parts were beneath the surface:

Outlook email triggers.

Subject filtering and data extraction.

SharePoint integration.

Excel table structures.

Metadata challenges.

Dynamic file paths.

Power Automate expressions.

Error handling.

Daily aggregation logic.

Validation workflows.

Email distribution processes.

At first, every solution seemed to create two new problems.

One moment it was a broken table reference.

The next it was metadata returning the wrong value.

Then a file path issue.

Then an Excel connector error.

Then something else and something else again.

There were moments when I genuinely thought:

"Why is this not working?"

But every problem taught me something new.

Every failure added another piece to the puzzle.

And slowly, the project started taking shape.

Three days into the project, I already had nearly half of the automation working.

Emails were being collected.

Data was being extracted.

Information was flowing into SharePoint and Excel.

For the first time, I could see the vision becoming reality.

And that's when something interesting happened.

The project stopped being about technology.

It became about problem-solving.

Because automation isn't really about Power Automate.

It's about understanding a process deeply enough that a computer can perform it for you.

You have to think differently.

You have to anticipate failures.

You have to understand where every piece of data comes from and where it goes next.

And that's exactly what I learned during this journey.

Two weeks later, the result is something I'm genuinely proud of:

✅ Automated email data collection

✅ Automated data extraction

✅ Automated SharePoint and Excel storage

✅ Daily summary calculations

✅ Validation workflow

✅ End-user notification process

✅ Blueprint documentation

✅ Workflow visualizations

✅ Presentation materials

✅ Fully tested end-to-end prototype

Today, I not only understand how it works, but I can explain it, present it, document it, and improve it.

One of the most important lessons I've learned is this:

Don't ask me how to build it.

Tell me what you want to achieve.

Tell me what process is causing frustration.

Tell me what you want to automate. 

And let me figure it out.

